PREFLIGHT
{"file":"HKF-118.pdf","title":"Adaptive Partition and Resource Allocation for Collaborative Inference Services in UAV Swarm","page":2,"section":"II-D. Problem Formulation","quote":"The objective of this paper is to obtain"}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines an energy-harvesting heterogeneous UAV swarm MEC system with I RUAVs and J SUAVs, formulating DNN partitioning and resource control as an MINLP and P-MDP.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II. SYETEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"comprising I multi-task receiving UAVs (RUAVs)"},{"page":3,"section":"III-A. P-MDP Formulation","quote":"We employ a P-MDP framework that utilizes historical"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly states FIFO queues, Poisson task/energy arrivals, discrete partition/offloading bounds, CPU/power/bandwidth limits, constant SUAV GPU frequency, and neglected SUAV computation delay.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II-A. Task Reception and DNN Partition Inference Model","quote":"task arrival process can be modeled as Poisson"},{"page":3,"section":"II-C. Computation Model","quote":"The computation delay at the SUAV edge is neglected."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Tables I–II specify ResNet-18 block loads, mobility bounds, radio/energy rates, and PPO hyperparameters, but cruise altitude H, simulator/hardware environment, and LSTM/FC hidden dimensions are omitted.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION, Table II","quote":"Number of test time slots N 3000"},{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"ResNet-18 can be divided into Pi = 5"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Evaluates LE-MHAPPO against only one baseline (MHAPPO without LSTM) under matched V and ΔT conditions; lacks non-RL, heuristic, or alternative MARL comparators.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"We compare the LE-MHAPPO and MHAPPO algorithms,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Evaluation metrics (reward, task delay, and total system energy consumption in Figs. 2–3) directly align with the weighted delay-energy objective Eq. (13a) and reward Eq. (21).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION, Fig. 2","quote":"(a) Reward; (b) Task Delay; (c) Total System"},{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION, Fig. 3","quote":"Across Various Metrics as a Function of V:"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Averages training over 10 seeds and tests over 5 pre-generated trajectories with sensitivity over V and ΔT, but reports no variance, standard deviation, or confidence intervals.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"different weight values of V, averaged over 10 seeds."},{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"tested on five pre-generated flight and task arrival"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Models 2D kinematic mobility with repulsive collision avoidance and Poisson energy harvesting with depletion-pause states, but fixes altitude H, uses free-space path loss Eq. (8), and omits link/UAV failures.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II. SYETEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"all UAVs adjust Vk(Tn) based on a repulsive"},{"page":3,"section":"II-D. Communication Model","quote":"average pass loss between RUAVi and SUAVj"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Conclusion accurately reflects simulation gains over MHAPPO, but claims support for actual UAV swarms despite simulation-only evaluation on one CNN (ResNet-18) and one baseline.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"V. CONCLUSION","quote":"action prediction requirements in actual UAV swarms,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Sections IV–V, Acknowledgment, and References (pp. 5–6); no author code repository, simulation scripts, or trained model links are provided.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"Additional environment and algorithm parameters for LH-MHAPPO are"}],"note":"Checked pp. 5-6; no author artifact URLs found."}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Funding grants and Technology Innovation Institute LLC support are disclosed in Acknowledgment (p. 6), but no explicit limitations or conflicts-of-interest statements are provided.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"ACKNOWLEDGMENT","quote":"National Key Research and Development Program of China"}],"note":"Checked V. Conclusion and Acknowledgment (p. 6); no limitations or COI statement."}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"outcome","key":"Q01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"LE-MHAPPO reduces the weighted sum of task delay and system energy consumption by at least 13.8% (13.8% in Abstract) vs MHAPPO, with performance degradation capped at 27.7% of MHAPPO across ΔT ∈ {1,2,3} s and V ∈ {0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9} (J=2 SUAVs, I=5 RUAVs, ResNet-18, 5 trajectories × 3000 slots).","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"with at least a 13.8% reduction in their"},{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"degradation remains capped at just 27.7% of the"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"VERIFIED_EXACT: Checked Abstract (p. 1) and Section IV (pp. 5–6); exact relative reductions (13.8%, 27.7%) are printed in prose, while absolute reward, delay, and energy curves in Figs. 2–3 are plot-only.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"with at least a 13.8% reduction in their"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decisions: SUAV bandwidth Wj, RUAV partition ci, offloading oi, CPU frequency fi, transmit power pi. Objective: minimize weighted sum of average task delay and energy Eq. (13a). Constraints: bandwidth sum, discrete ci/oi, frequency/power bounds. Solver: CTDE MARL LE-MHAPPO (LSTM+PPO).","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"II-E. Problem formulation","quote":"minimize the weighted sum of the average task"},{"page":4,"section":"III-A. P-MDP Formulation","quote":"processed through a Tstep-layer LSTM to extract"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t2_partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Identical pre-trained DNNs stored on RUAVs and SUAVs; ResNet-18 partitioned sequentially into Pi=5 blocks via single split point ci(Tn) ∈ {0..5}. RUAV executes front blocks, compresses intermediate data, and offloads remaining blocks to selected SUAV oi(Tn) ∈ J.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II-A. Task Reception and DNN Partition Inference Model","quote":"Both RUAVs and SUAVs store identical pre-trained DNNs."},{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"ResNet-18 can be divided into Pi = 5"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t2_topology","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Two-tier heterogeneous aerial MEC swarm: I=5 multi-task receiving UAVs (RUAVs) and J=2 GPU-equipped multi-server UAVs (SUAVs) cruising at fixed altitude H over 500 m × 500 m. CTDE execution: centralized critic during training, distributed S-actors (SUAVs) and R-actors (RUAVs) online.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II. SYETEM MODEL AND PROBLEM FORMULATION","quote":"comprising I multi-task receiving UAVs (RUAVs)"},{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"simulation includes J = 2 SUAVs and I = 5"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t2_workload","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Model/task: ResNet-18 (5 blocks, Table I) for image recognition; synthetic Poisson task arrivals at λT=5 tasks/s over N=3000 slots; no real image dataset named. Baseline: MHAPPO without LSTM (p. 6).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"We use the ResNet-18 architecture for image recognition"},{"page":6,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"We compare the LE-MHAPPO and MHAPPO algorithms,"}],"note":"No image dataset name reported."}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Mobility: 2D fixed-altitude kinematics with repulsive collision/boundary avoidance (vmax=10 m/s, amax=2 m/s², θmax=30°). Radio: OFDM, Wmax=6 MHz, fc=2 GHz, N0=-106 dBm, free-space path loss Eq. (8). Events: solar Poisson energy harvesting (λE=2,4 J/s) with SLEI/SLEO pause states; no UAV failure or link-outage recovery.","evidence":[{"page":2,"section":"II-B. Energy Harvesting Model","quote":"pauses local computation due to energy depletion."},{"page":3,"section":"II-D. Communication Model","quote":"facilitated by Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiplexing (OFDM)."}],"note":"No UAV failure/link-drop recovery modeled."}
{"id":"HKF-064","kind":"technical","key":"t3_platform_modality","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Custom simulation-only evaluation over 5 pre-generated random flight/task trajectories (N=3000 slots, 500 m × 500 m); simulator software, compute hardware, and real traces are not reported.","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"IV. SIMULATION RESULTS AND DISCUSSION","quote":"tested on five pre-generated flight and task arrival"}],"note":"Simulator tool/hardware not reported."}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines a wireless ad hoc N-UAV swarm executing distributed CNN inference over U2U and U2G links, formulating latency minimization with operability constraints as an ILP and MDP solved by PPO (DRL-RLD).","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"2. System Model","quote":"UAV swarms are configured to form a wireless"},{"page":7,"section":"4. Deep Reinforcement Learning Model for Layer Distribution","quote":"The DRL-RLD model is built using a Markov"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly states fixed altitude z=40 m, non-residual sequential CNN assumption, rural LoS/NLoS path loss, memory/compute limits, single-UAV layer assignment, collision-free cell occupancy, and inoperable-UAV exclusion constraints Eqs. (11a–11f).","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"2. System Model","quote":"compatible with CNN architectures that do not incorporate"},{"page":6,"section":"3. Problem Formulation","quote":"ensures that no layers are assigned to inoperable"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Section 5.1 and Tables 1–2 list grid size, UAV count, memory/FLOPS, CNN depth/params, and PPO rates, but omit carrier frequency f, reward constant A, simulator/hardware, PPO network architecture, and units for N0=4×10^-21.","evidence":[{"page":10,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings, Table 2","quote":"noise power spectral density 4 ×10−21"},{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"The UAVs are assumed to perform the mission"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Compares DRL-RLD against three baselines (Layer-Avg, Comp-Avg, Heuristic) under matched conditions (15 UAVs, 1 GB RAM, 11.2 GFLOPS) across AlexNet, YOLO, and VGG16, and under 0–2 inoperable UAVs (AlexNet).","evidence":[{"page":10,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"evaluated under the same conditions, including fifteen UAVs,"},{"page":11,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"three conditions: all UAVs fully operational, one inoperable"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Primary evaluation metrics (episode reward in Fig. 3 and end-to-end latency in Figs. 4–6) directly match the ILP objective Eq. (11) and inverse-latency reward Eq. (16).","evidence":[{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"The end-to-end latency is employed to evaluate the"},{"page":11,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"lower the end-to-end latency than the three baseline"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Conducts sensitivity analyses over swarm size (5/10/15), requests (2–10), memory (1/2 GB), and inoperable UAVs (0–2), but reports no multi-seed repetitions, variance, or confidence intervals.","evidence":[{"page":10,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"as the number of UAVs in the swarm"},{"page":12,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation, Fig. 5","quote":"Performance comparison of the proposed system under different"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Models 3D rural LoS/NLoS path loss with U2U/U2G links and 0–2 random inoperable UAVs, but fixes altitude z=40 m on a 25-cell grid and omits mobility trajectories, Doppler, obstacles, weather, and battery limits.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"2. System Model, Communications Model for U2U and U2G Link","quote":"under the assumption of a rural environment with"},{"page":13,"section":"6. Conclusions","quote":"accounts for the Doppler effect is necessary to"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Conclusions align with simulated latency and reallocation gains, though claims of real-time adaptive mission execution and generalization rest solely on simplified simulations without physical tests or printed numeric margins.","evidence":[{"page":12,"section":"6. Conclusions","quote":"The simulation results proved that the proposed system"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Section 5.1 and Data Availability Statement (pp. 9, 13); only third-party benchmark datasets (ILSVRC 2014, PASCAL VOC 2007) are linked, with no author code or simulation artifacts.","evidence":[{"page":13,"section":"Data Availability Statement","quote":"Publicly available datasets were analyzed in this study."}],"note":"Third-party dataset links only; no author code/models."}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly discloses training instability at larger swarm sizes (p. 10), environmental/Doppler/battery limitations (pp. 12–13), IITP/Kwangwoon funding (p. 13), and Youngghyu Sun's SMART EVER employment in Conflicts of Interest (p. 13).","evidence":[{"page":10,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"instability in the early stages of learning increases"},{"page":13,"section":"Conflicts of Interest","quote":"Author Youngghyu Sun was employed by the company"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"PLOT_ONLY: Checked Section 5.2 (pp. 10–12) and Tables 1–2 (pp. 9–10); all observed reward and latency outcomes appear solely in Figs. 3–6 plots with no printed outcome numbers in text or tables.","evidence":[{"page":11,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation, Fig. 4","quote":"The performance of the proposed model compared to"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decisions: binary layer assignment δt,r,i,j (plus cell placement ψt,i,q in ILP; action is swarm layer allocation in MDP). Objective: minimize end-to-end U2U+U2G+compute latency Eq. (11). Constraints: memory, compute, single-assignment, cell collision, operability. Solver: offline-trained PPO (DRL-RLD).","evidence":[{"page":5,"section":"3. Problem Formulation","quote":"proposed optimization problem can be an integer linear"},{"page":8,"section":"4.2. Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithm","quote":"Proximal policy optimization (PPO) [32] is one of"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t2_partition","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Every UAV stores a full copy of the trained non-residual CNN (L sequential conv/FCN layers) and executes only DRL-assigned active layers in a pipeline, transmitting intermediate outputs Kj between UAVs and final output Kr,L to ground.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"2. System Model","quote":"Each UAV in the swarm contains a copy"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t2_topology","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Wireless ad hoc swarm of N UAVs (5, 10, 15) over a 100 m × 100 m 25-cell grid at fixed z=40 m with one hot cell and a ground device (U2U and U2G links). Centralized/single-agent offline PPO policy outputs swarm-wide layer allocation.","evidence":[{"page":3,"section":"2. System Model","quote":"network consists of N UAVs, which are employed"},{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"operate in a 100 m ×100 m area,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t2_workload","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Models: AlexNet (74.7M, 8 layers), VGG16 (169.8M, 16 layers), YOLO (295.5M, 20 layers); trained references ILSVRC 2014 and PASCAL VOC 2007; 2–10 parallel requests. Baselines: Layer-Avg, Comp-Avg, Heuristic.","evidence":[{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"Three CNN models were employed in the simulation:"},{"page":10,"section":"5.2. Performance Evaluation","quote":"The Layer-Avg method [24] refers to the method"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t3_network_event_response","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Mobility: grid cells at fixed z=40 m, Gaussian-initialized positions (no flight dynamics). Radio: rural LoS/NLoS path loss (a=4.88, b=0.43, ηNLoS=20 dB), Shannon rate (Pt=0.1 W, B=1 kHz, N0=4×10^-21; fc missing). Events: 0–2 random inoperable UAVs trigger real-time layer reallocation.","evidence":[{"page":4,"section":"2. System Model","quote":"reallocates layers based on the current state of"},{"page":10,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings, Table 2","quote":"transmission power 0.1 W"}],"note":"Carrier frequency f and dynamic flight trajectories omitted."}
{"id":"HKF-022","kind":"technical","key":"t3_platform_modality","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"Simulation-only evaluation (1000 episodes in Fig. 3; UAVs modeled at 1–2 GB RAM, 11.2 GFLOPS); simulator software, host hardware, and real aerial traces are not reported.","evidence":[{"page":9,"section":"5.1. Simulation Settings","quote":"one computational capacity level (11.2 GFLOPS)."}],"note":"Simulator framework and host hardware not reported."}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Clearly defines an edge-only heterogeneous UAV swarm LLM inference problem and formulates Orion's three components: State-Compressed DP layer partitioning, Min-Max DP adaptive sequence partitioning, and predictive decoding.","evidence":[{"page":6,"section":"3. Proposed Solution: The Orion Framework","quote":"we propose Orion, an edge-only collaborative inference framework"},{"page":7,"section":"3.1.1. System Model and Problem Formulation","quote":"minimizes the maximum latency (computation plus communication) across"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Explicitly states edge-only filtering (no cloud), memory capacity limits, non-recurrent device pipeline constraint, quadratic causal-attention cost model, minimum chunk length constraint, and speculative rollback assumptions.","evidence":[{"page":7,"section":"3.1.1. System Model and Problem Formulation","quote":"Data offloading to the cloud is strictly prohibited."},{"page":11,"section":"3.2.2. Min-Max DP for Sequence Partitioning","quote":"enforce a chunk length constraint y−l ≥ min"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D03","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Section 4.1 and Table 1 specify Python 3.13.5, emulated hardware TFLOPS/RAM, bandwidths, Llama-2 7B/13B/70B (FP16), and sequence lengths, but omit min_len, max_seq, exact per-layer coefficients for 13B/70B/Orin NX, and prediction-head details.","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"4.1. Experimental Setup, Table 1","quote":"NVIDIA Jetson AGX Orin (1.88 TFLOPS, 16 GB RAM)"},{"page":16,"section":"4.1. Experimental Setup, Table 1. Cont.","quote":"Llama-2 7B, 13B, 70B (FP16)"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D04","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Compares Orion against five baselines (Edge-Solo, Cloud-Edge-Even, Cloud-Edge-Opt, EdgeShard, Jupiter) under matched emulated hardware, sequence lengths, and network bandwidth sweeps across Sections 4.2–4.5.","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"4.1. Experimental Setup","quote":"We compare Orion against 5 baselines to demonstrate"},{"page":18,"section":"4.4. Latency on Llama-2 13B of Varying Air-to-Ground Bandwidths","quote":"evaluated performance under varying air-to-ground bandwidths (100, 150,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D05","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Primary prefill latency metric directly aligns with the pipeline-bottleneck objective Eqs. (4) and (11), but generation quality/output accuracy of LLM reasoning and real speculative-head accuracy on sensor traces are not empirically evaluated.","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"4.1. Experimental Setup","quote":"Prefill latency was the primary metric."},{"page":10,"section":"3.2.1. Quadratic Inference Cost Model for Causal Attention","quote":"Theoretical derivation (used in our simulation): Based on"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D06","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Sections 4.7–4.8 use 300 and 100 Monte Carlo trials (reporting CV=0.067, match rate, degradation) alongside broad parameter/ablation sweeps, but primary Sections 4.2–4.6 omit repetition counts and dispersion.","evidence":[{"page":20,"section":"4.7. Investigation of Prediction Accuracy on End-to-End Latency","quote":"performed 300 Monte Carlo simulations for each configuration"},{"page":21,"section":"4.8. Robustness Analysis Under Environmental Uncertainty","quote":"with N= 100 independent trials executed for each."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D07","status":"PARTIAL","finding":"PARTIAL: Emulates ±20% inter-UAV bandwidth fluctuation and Gaussian compute/comm noise (§4.8), but lacks physical UAV mobility trajectories, wireless channel/fading/MAC contention, packet loss, and hardware testbed validation (deferred in §5.5).","evidence":[{"page":15,"section":"4.1. Experimental Setup","quote":"±20% fluctuation applied to all bandwidth settings to"},{"page":23,"section":"5.5. Real-System Deployment and Physical Validation","quote":"implement Orion on a physical UAV testbed (e.g.,"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D08","status":"NO","finding":"NO: Highlights, Abstract, and Section 1 claim Orion is the only framework capable of running 70B on memory-constrained UAV nodes, contradicting Section 4.2 (p. 17) where Jupiter (27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (43,058 ms) also succeed; Section 4.6 (16,793 ms) also differs from Section 4.2 (18,665 ms) for 70B.","evidence":[{"page":1,"section":"Abstract","quote":"Orion is the only framework capable of running"},{"page":17,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"significantly outperforming Jupiter (27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (43,058 ms)."}],"note":"Internal contradiction between Abstract/Intro and Section 4.2."}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D09","status":"NOT_REPORTED","finding":"NOT_REPORTED: Checked Section 4.1 and Data Availability Statement (pp. 14, 24); no public code repository, simulation scripts, or sensor trace datasets are released (inquiries directed to corresponding author).","evidence":[{"page":24,"section":"Data Availability Statement","quote":"further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author."}],"note":"No public author artifacts."}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"appraisal","key":"D10","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"YES: Section 5 (pp. 22–23) discusses decoding, uncertainty, text-only multi-modal, and physical-testbed limitations; Funding (NSFC 62402450, Zhejiang LQ24F020024) and Conflicts of Interest (none) are declared on p. 24.","evidence":[{"page":23,"section":"5.4. Extension to Multi-Modal Sensor Data","quote":"While Orion currently processes textual sensor logs, real-world"},{"page":24,"section":"Conflicts of Interest","quote":"The authors declare no conflicts of interest."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"outcome","key":"Q01","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Prefill inference latency on Llama-2 7B (64-token input, 1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G): Orion achieved 8864 ms (81% reduction vs Cloud-Edge-Opt 46,522 ms; faster than Jupiter 11,260 ms and EdgeShard 24,629 ms; slightly slower than Edge-Solo 8047 ms).","evidence":[{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"Orion achieved a latency of 8864 ms, representing"},{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"outperforms distributed baselines Jupiter (11,260 ms) and EdgeShard"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"outcome","key":"Q02","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Inference latency at Llama-2 13B scale (64-token input, 1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G): Orion achieved 11,824 ms (78% reduction vs Cloud-Edge-Opt 53,166 ms; vs Cloud-Edge-Even 54,485 ms, EdgeShard 29,045 ms, Jupiter 16,870 ms; Edge-Solo OOM).","evidence":[{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"Orion successfully executed inference at 11,824 ms, not"},{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"clear lead over Jupiter (16,870 ms) and EdgeShard"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"outcome","key":"Q03","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Inference latency at Llama-2 70B scale (64-token input, 1000 Mb/s inter-UAV, 200 Mb/s A2G): Orion achieved 18,665 ms vs Jupiter 27,264 ms and EdgeShard 43,058 ms (cloud-edge baselines failed due to memory constraints; note §4.6 ablation reports 16,793 ms).","evidence":[{"page":17,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"most efficient with an 18,665 ms latency, significantly"},{"page":17,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"significantly outperforming Jupiter (27,264 ms) and EdgeShard (43,058 ms)."}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"report_coverage","key":"coverage","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"VERIFIED_EXACT: Checked Sections 4.2–4.8 (pp. 16–21); exact numerical latency, ablation, break-even (8.94%), and robustness (CV 0.067, 9.3%, 12.7%) outcomes are printed in body prose alongside Figs. 4–10.","evidence":[{"page":16,"section":"4.2. Latency on Llama-2 Models of Varying Scales","quote":"Orion achieved a latency of 8864 ms, representing"}],"note":""}
{"id":"HKF-104","kind":"technical","key":"t2_decisions_objective_solver","status":"VERIFIED","finding":"Decisions: contiguous layer-to-device mapping P (Algorithm 1), non-uniform sequence chunking P* (Algorithm 2), and speculative first-token prediction. Objective: minimize bottleneck pipeline latency Eqs. (4), (11). Constraints: edge-only filter, device memory Cs, non-recurrent devices, min_len. Solver: State-Compressed DP + Min-Max DP.","evidence":[{"page":8,"section":"3.1.2. Joint Optimization via Dynamic Programming","quote":"solved utilizing dynamic programming with state compression (State-Compressed"},{"page":11,"section":"3.2.2. Min-Max DP for Sequence Partitioning","quote":"we design a Min-Max Dynamic Programming (DP) algorithm."}],"note":""}
